<!-- include-defer-mode.zul

	When is defer mode the right choice?

	Defer mode is the only mode that can reach a servlet. Include#include() calls
	Execution#include(), which calls Servlets.include(), which calls
	RequestDispatcher#include() — so the target is served by the container, with
	its filters, its own request, and the query string of src as real request
	parameters.

	Instant mode never leaves ZK: Include#afterCompose() calls
	Execution#createComponents() on a ZUML page definition and composes the result
	as its own children. There is no request, so a servlet cannot be reached and a
	query string cannot be honoured.

	Run: mvn jetty:run
	Open: http://localhost:8080/component/essential/include-defer-mode.zul -->
<zk xmlns:h="native">
	<style>
		.report { padding: .5em 1em; }
		.report dt { font-family: monospace; color: #555; margin-top: .4em; }
		.report dd { margin: 0 0 0 1.5em; font-weight: bold; }
	</style>

	<h:h2>1. Defer mode reaches a servlet, and the query string survives</h:h2>
	<h:p>
		The <h:code>src</h:code> below points at a plain servlet, not at a ZUML page.
		Only defer mode can render it. <h:code>auto</h:code> already picks defer here,
		for two independent reasons: the extension is not a ZUML one, and a query
		string is present.
	</h:p>
	<groupbox mold="3d">
		<include src="/sales-report?region=EMEA&amp;year=2026"
				 currency="EUR"/>
	</groupbox>

	<h:h2>2. The same fragment, both modes, same query string</h:h2>
	<h:p>
		Both includes below name the same ZUML fragment with the same query string.
		Only the deferred one is loaded through a request of its own, so only it sees
		<h:code>param.region</h:code>. The instant one drops the query string and logs
		<h:i>"Query string is not allowed in instant mode"</h:i> on the server console.
	</h:p>
	<grid>
		<columns>
			<column label="mode" width="120px"/>
			<column label="what the fragment sees"/>
		</columns>
		<rows>
			<row>
				<label value="instant"/>
				<include mode="instant" src="include-region-fragment.zul?region=EMEA&amp;year=2026"/>
			</row>
			<row>
				<label value="defer"/>
				<include mode="defer" src="include-region-fragment.zul?region=EMEA&amp;year=2026"/>
			</row>
		</rows>
	</grid>

	<h:h2>3. The same thing with a JSP</h:h2>
	<h:p>
		A JSP is the classic case for defer mode. It behaves exactly like the servlet
		above, because it <h:i>is</h:i> a servlet: the container compiles it, runs it for
		this request, and it reads its input from the request. The
		<h:code>served by</h:code> line below is the generated servlet class.
	</h:p>
	<groupbox mold="3d">
		<include src="sales-report.jsp?region=EMEA&amp;year=2026" currency="EUR"/>
	</groupbox>
</zk>
